'''''Sisyrinchium''''' is a large [[genus]] of [[annual plant|annual]] to [[perennial plant|perennial]] [[flowering plant|flowering plants]] in the family [[Iridaceae]]. Native to the [[New World]], the [[species]] are known as '''blue-eyed grasses.''' Although they are not true grasses (Poaceae), they are [[monocot]]s.{{sfn|Southeastern Flora|2025}}

Several species in the eastern [[United States]] are threatened or endangered. This may be due to niche species emerging from their unique tendency toward rapid [[speciation]] which helps them adapt to specialized arenic (sandy) habitats.{{sfn|Keith|2022|p=1}}

==Description==
{{unreferenced section|date=June 2014}}
[[Image:Sisyrinchium angustifolium RHu 002.JPG|thumb|''[[Sisyrinchium angustifolium]]'']]
[[Image:Sisyrinchium californicum 7.jpg|thumb|''[[Sisyrinchium californicum]]'']]
[[Image:Sisyrinchium albidum.jpg|thumb|''[[Sisyrinchium albidum]]'']]

These are not true [[grass]]es, but many species have the general appearance of grasses, as they are low-growing plants with long, thin leaves. They often grow on [[grassland]]s. Many species resemble [[Iris (plant)|iris]]es, to which they are more closely related. Most species grow as [[perennial plant]]s, from a [[rhizome]], though some are short-lived (e.g. ''[[Sisyrinchium striatum]])'', and some are [[Annual plant|annuals]] (e.g. ''[[Sisyrinchium micranthum|Sisyrinchium iridifolium]]'').

The flowers are relatively simple and often grow in clusters.

Many species, particularly the [[South America|South American]] ones, are not blue, despite the common name. The genus includes species with blue, white, yellow, and purple petals, often with a contrasting centre. Of the species in the United States, the [[Sisyrinchium bellum|western blue-eyed grass]] ''(Sisyrinchium bellum'') is sometimes found with white flowers, while the [[Sisyrinchium californicum|California golden-eyed grass]] (''Sisyrinchium californicum'') has yellow flowers.

==Taxonomy==
{{refimprove section|date=June 2014}}
The genus was named by [[Carl Linnaeus]] in 1753, based on the species ''[[Sisyrinchium bermudiana]]'' (commonly called ''Bermudiana''). The taxonomy of this genus is rather perplexing and confusing,{{to whom|date=September 2012}} as several of these species, such as ''[[Sisyrinchium angustifolium]]'', form complexes with many variants named as species. More genetic research and [[Cladistics|cladistic]] analysis need to be performed to sort out the relationships between the species. Some species, notably ''[[Olsynium douglasii|Sisyrinchium douglasii]]'', have been transferred to the separate genus ''[[Olsynium]]''. 

The greatest diversity for the genus is found in South America.{{sfn|Chauveau|Eggers|Raquin|Silvério|2011|p=1288}}

Approximately one third of the species in the genus have oil producing hairs called [[elaiophore]]s to attract oil-bees. Nearly all these species are native to South America.{{sfn|Chauveau|Eggers|Raquin|Silvério|2011}}

== Etymology ==
The genus name ''Sisyrinchium'' appears to be derived from ''Sisyrinchíon'', the Ancient Greek name for the Barbary nut (''[[Moraea sisyrinchium]]''), a common Mediterranean plant of southern [[Europe]] and [[North Africa|northern Africa]]. The Greek name, recorded by [[Pliny the Elder|Pliny]] and [[Theophrastus]], refers to the way the [[corm]] tunics of that plant resemble a shaggy [[goat]]'s-hair coat, ''sisýra''.<ref>{{LSJ|sisurigxi/on|σισυριγχίον}}, {{LSJ|sisu/ra|σισύρα|shortref}}</ref> As Goldblatt and Manning explain, "the reason for applying the name to a genus of New World Iridaceae was apparently arbitrary."{{sfn|Manning|Goldblatt|2008|p=221}}

As an alternative, authors as early Giacinto Ambrosini in as 1666{{sfn|Ambrosini|1666|p=503}} give the dubious [[etymology]] of [[Latin]] {{Lang|la|sūs}} "pig" and Greek ''rhynchos'' "nose", referring to [[pig]]s grubbing the roots.

== Selected species ==
{{main|List of Sisyrinchium species}}
There are 212 accepted species {{asof|2025|lc=y}},{{sfn|POWO|2025}} including:
{{div col|colwidth=45em}}
* ''[[Sisyrinchium albidum]]'' <small>Raf.</small>
* ''[[Sisyrinchium angustifolium]]'' <small>[[Philip Miller|Mill.]]</small> – narrow-leaf blue-eyed-grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium atlanticum]]'' <small>E.P.Bicknel</small> – eastern blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium bellum]]'' <small>S.Watson</small> – western blue-eyed grass or Californian blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium bermudiana]]'' – bermudiana
* ''[[Sisyrinchium californicum]]'' – golden blue-eyed grass, yellow-eyed-grass, and golden-eyed-grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium campestre]]'' <small>Bickn.</small>
* ''[[Sisyrinchium dichotomum]]'' – wishbone blue-eyed grass, white irisette, and reflexed blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium elmeri]]'' – Elmer's blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium funereum]]'' – Funeral Mountain blue-eyed grass and Death Valley blue-eyed-grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium groenlandicum]]'' – Greenland blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium halophilum]]'' – Nevada blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium idahoense]]'' <small>Bickn.</small> – Idaho blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium longipes]]'' – timberland blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium micranthum]]'' – annual blue-eyed grass, blue pigroot, fairy stars, and striped rush-leaf
* ''[[Sisyrinchium montanum]]'' <small>Greene</small> – strict blue-eyed grass, American blue-eyed-grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium platense]]'' <small>I.M.Johnst</small>
* ''[[Sisyrinchium pruinosum]]'' – spotted blue-eyed grass or dotted blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium sarmentosum]]'' – mountain blue-eyed grass and pale blue-eyed-grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium septentrionale]]'' – northern blue-eyed grass
* ''[[Sisyrinchium striatum]]'' – pale yellow-eyed-grass or satin flower
{{div col end}}

